edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510364 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02febb14-e263-5cc1-8053-2e425b9d6cc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510364 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4(5) & =-11 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510447 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} & -5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c07b8d08-df25-5edc-8755-7d7271698aeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510447 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{70}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 3\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -10\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-10.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}(-10) & =-10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}{x}_{1} & =10\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510449 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510449 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510449 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510449 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nx={R}^{-1}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3b9fdd0-8dab-5a0b-9e88-9a8e531bca1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510449 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is ${Q}^{T}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510454 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nx={R}^{-1}{Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb3d3dae-b723-59f7-8d1b-568e2e8664df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510454 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510486 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510486 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c7fbdd9-d70e-53c6-9583-045def1fb610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510486 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510507 #edb/ref